NEST:
    NestJS is built around one core idea: every piece of your app is organized into modules. Each module has three kinds of files:
        * Controller -> receives HTTP requests, sends responses
        * Service -> contains the actual business logic (functions-like)
        * Module -> wires the controller and service together
    /src Files explained:
        * main.ts: The very first file that runs. It boots up the entire application, creates the NestJS app, sets the port, and starts listening for requests.
                   You only have one of these in the whole project.
        * app.module.ts: The root of the entire app, contains all the modules, controllers, and services, NestJS reads this file to know the app's structure.
        * app.controller.ts: Handles incoming HTTP requests. Defines your routes like: GET /, POST /users etc. It does not contain logic,
                             it just receives the request and calls the service.
        * app.service.ts: contains the actual logic (functions) that the controller calls.
        * app.controller.spec.ts: contains tests for the controller, i will ignore this file for now, as i am not able yet to write tests.